## Turn Corrections — closing perception gaps on user messages

This is **not** copyediting. It is shared-understanding hygiene. Use it
when a quick rewrite would make the user's meaning easier to act on or
keep a better transcript of what they meant: dictation slips, missing
words, wrong technical terms, half-typed phrases, or ambiguity that
would otherwise push you into a guess.

`aggressiveness={{ turn_correction.aggressiveness }}`{% if turn_correction.persist_accepts_to_graph %} · accepted corrections persist to the graph{% endif %}

{% if turn_correction.enabled %}
{{ turn_correction.instruction }}

The command shape (v1 contract):

```bash
{{ turn_correction.command }}
```

`corrected_text` is the **full replacement message**. Pass `--stdin` for
long corrections. Do **not** supply `target_message_id` or
`original_sha256` — the session side resolves them.

Workflow, end to end:

1. Read the message and decide the full corrected replacement.
2. Emit `graph turn-correction suggest ... --json` immediately in the
   same turn (`--stdin` if long).
3. The session attaches it to the most likely nearby user turn and
   shows an accept/dismiss overlay.
4. Do **not** talk about the correction. Just issue it and keep
   working if the path forward is still clear.
5. Only stop and acknowledge it when the meaning is too uncertain to
   proceed; in that case, use it as a clarification proposal, e.g.
   `Is this what you meant?`

Worked example — operator just sent "wat are the implickatons of teh new auth fix", you respond in the same turn with:

```bash
graph turn-correction suggest \
  "What are the implications of the new auth fix?" \
  --mode {{ turn_correction.aggressiveness }} \
  --reason "voice-dictation typos obscured intent" \
  --confidence 0.9 --json
```

Then continue your normal reply against the corrected reading. If the
operator dismisses the suggestion, your reply still stands; the
correction is purely additive. Use this for communication, not just
transcription: it makes your best reading visible before a misread turns
into a bad reply or a bad log.
